## MUST A — the ladder routes a playwright run before the TAP clause

Accepted in full; the prereview's diagnosis was right and its fix shape is the
one adopted. Revision 1 put the unanchored token clause third, so it stopped the
ladder: a real three-failure transcript plus one echoed `not ok 1 - setup` line,
or an `AssertionError` out of the `bisellium serve` the W-110 harness spawns, was
accepted as one behaviour's red with its count and matcher unchecked. I
reproduced both acceptances from `probe-clause3.mjs` before changing anything.

The ladder now asks **"is this a playwright run?"** at clause 3 — the last
`PW_SUMMARY` match, or a new `PW_BANNER`
`/^[ \t]*Running \d+ tests? using \d+ workers?\b/m` — and a yes routes
unconditionally through the count and matcher clauses, never reaching the token
clause, which moves to 7 and keeps today's semantics for a non-playwright run
exactly. The union of summary and banner rather than either alone, for a reason
I measured: `launch` keeps only the **last** 4 MiB of each stream, so a long run
can lose its banner off the head while keeping its summary at the tail, and a run
that died before summarising still printed its banner. The union fails closed in
both directions; banner-only routing would fall back to the token clause, which
is the unsafe direction.

The banner regex is measured, not assumed: `Running N test(s) using M worker(s)`
is the first non-blank line under `list`, `line` **and** `dot`.

Retained revision-2 kit, `measure-replay-acceptance-2.mjs` with
`measurement-2.txt`: **22 transcripts, 0 disagreements** — revision 1's thirteen,
the prereview's three adversarial cases, and six I constructed for the routing
clause. Cases B and C now refuse with `playwright reported 3 failed tests`; the
converse is pinned too (case F: a genuine one-failure transcript with an
`AssertionError` appended from a spawned server is still accepted, so the routing
refuses the near miss without breaking the real red). Revision 1's kit still
reports `0 disagreements` and is kept as the round-1 record.

One verdict moves in the refusing direction, and the brief states it rather than
discovering it in review: a **non**-playwright transcript that prints `N failed`
at line start is routed to the playwright branch and refused (case J). Measured,
nothing in this repo does it — `node --test-reporter=tap` summarises as
`# fail N`, and none of W-110's six TAP reds matches the anchor, which is why all
six keep their verdict. The failure is fail-closed, the reason names it, and a
refusal is recoverable by re-recording where a false acceptance would be
permanent. *Out of scope* now forbids narrowing the routing to close it.

New rows: 4(k) is the combined not-ok-echo plus playwright-summary case the
prereview asked for, with its converse; 4(l) covers banner-without-summary and a
passing transcript at nonzero status; 4(m) pins both sides of the line-start
hazard; 4(n) loops the whole table twice, which is the assertion that catches a
`lastIndex` leak. 5(d) asserts the same transcript through the real runner.

## MUST B — the 19th environment name is deterministic

Accepted, and the prereview's preferred shape taken: one sentinel browsers
directory pinned for **every** live row. Implementing it exposed one thing the
finding did not name, which is why *Files owned* grows by a file:
`builder-run.ts:227-231` hands the host runner a **closed** environment —
`PATH`, `LANG`, `LC_ALL`, `TZ`, and `TMPDIR` when set — with no `HOME` and no
`PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`. So a sentinel in `RUNNER_ENV` alone reaches only the
`runnerRun` path; the smoke row at `:628` goes through `run(...)`, where nothing
a test sets in `process.env` arrives at all. The sentinel is therefore pinned in
**both** places — `RUNNER_ENV` and `withTmp`, which is already the file's own
save/set/restore pattern for `TMPDIR` — and `builder-run.ts` gains one
conditional-spread line in the same shape that line already uses for `TMPDIR`,
forwarding an **absolute** `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`. That line is also the
production seam an operator needs in order to say where the browsers are; it is a
host-to-host environment, not a cell environment, and no cell gains a name from
it. Stated as such in *Files owned*, with the measurement behind it.

The brief's own contradiction is resolved rather than restated: the
"keep every existing row" clause now names exactly three permitted pre-existing
edits — `:670`'s count, the sentinel in the two helpers, and the `--behaviour`
bound — and says no pre-existing *row* changes its logic, fixture or any other
assertion. Behaviour 2(f) asserts that the runner resolved the **sentinel** and
not `~/.cache/ms-playwright`, so the count holds on a runner with no browsers
installed, and Acceptance now carries a bullet saying a row green here and red on
CI is not acceptance.

## SHOULD C, D, E

**C — gate cell.** Restated on the durable ground the prereview supplied: that
cell is already self-sufficient for a build (`copyModules` at `:817`, `npm` at
the head of its `PATH` at `:835`, writable `/candidate` and `/tmp`), which is
exactly what the replay cell lacked; the only thing it would still lack is a
browser, and nothing it runs today needs one. **Two named triggers** now put it
in scope — the first `kind: ui` record, and W-110's own 8(d), which makes
`/vite|run build/` match `test:suite` the day W-110 lands — so the next reader
who re-greps finds the trigger recorded instead of concluding the exclusion was
never measured. Both are carried in the second follow-on filing.

**D — the matcher clause.** Reworded to what it is: a discriminator against
*accidental* misclassification — a browser that would not launch, a server that
would not answer — and not a guarantee against an authored transcript. The brief
now says which case 4(i) closes (the code-frame echo, defeated by playwright's
`>  4 | ` gutter and by `expect(page.locator('…` failing the `)`), and concedes
the deliberate line-start `console.log` outright, citing W-125's own model: the
host reads output the candidate shapes. No unforgeable banner is claimed.

**E — `matchAll`.** Specified, with the reason: `PW_SUMMARY` is the one `/g`
regex, it is read only through `[...output.matchAll(PW_SUMMARY)]`, never
`.test()` or `.exec()`, and the match is taken once at the top of the playwright
branch and reused rather than re-matched per clause. 4(n)'s double pass over the
table is the check that fails if a builder substitutes `.exec()`.

**Prereview note on the 4 KiB build dump.** Taken: the brief now states the
asymmetry with the token-only discipline deliberately — clause 2's reason is
quoted into review logs so it carries no path, while the build dump is
cell-internal output from a cell with no credentials and no host paths, and L-051
wants a preparation failure a human can act on.

Signed as ready for a builder. No implementation, evidence or review verdict is
claimed by this signature; D-014's censor supplies the single independent
implementation review, judging this as a change to the W-125 trust boundary
(D-035).
